{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>וְאִם עֵז וגו׳.</b> In Torat Kohanim they said: He interrupted the matter, so that the goat does not require the fat tail — end of quote. And one needs to know in what respect He interrupted the matter. And Ba'al Korban Aharon explained from the fact that it should have said ״ואם עז״ but it said ״אם עז״. But his words are mistaken, for ״ואם עז״ is what is written." "And the RiVa (Tosafot Pesachim 96b) explained: since He did not include it together with the lamb, etc., and did not change anything in it, etc. And the matter is straightforward to me, that the intention of the Tanna is because we find that the verse said in the section of the burnt offering ״ואם מן הצאן וגו׳ מן הכבשים ומן העזים״ (Leviticus 1:10), whereas here in the section of peace offerings He interrupted the matter and said ״מן הצאן״ at the beginning and afterward returned to specify the law of the goat separately, to say that there is a new law in it." "And as for the fact that it did not say ״אם עז״ — you have learned that it relies on the first matter in all the details of the laws recorded when it says זבח שלמים זכר ונקבה וגו׳, for they are many — all of them are present in the peace offering of a goat, and the section of the goat was interrupted only for the fat tail. And the reason is because the verse returned to specify all the details of the fats and omitted the fat tail — this tells us that the fat tail distinguishes between goat and lamb." "And know that it was necessary to exclude the fat tail only for a goat, but for cattle, even though it said ״ואם כבש״ and Scripture combined them, it was not necessary to exclude the fat tail. And the reason is that in any event the tail of cattle is not like the tail of sheep and is not called אליה. Therefore, even though the verse combines two laws together — ״ואם מן הצאן״ — to apply what is said of this one to that one, that is only regarding a matter that exists in reality; but the fat tail — since the tail of cattle is not called אליה, whence would we have to include it that Scripture would need to exclude it? Therefore, even though the verse did not exclude it, they said in Torat Kohanim that it does not have the law of the fat tail — and specifically for a goat, because He wrote and said ״ואם מן הצאן״ and a goat is called צאן, so it might enter your mind to say that the verse said the fat tail about both of them. For this reason the verse needed to separate and say ״אם כשב אם עז״ וגו׳:"], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "The Or HaChaim refutes Ba'al Korban Aharon's claim that the text lacks a vav before אם; the actual biblical text (Leviticus 3:12) does read ואם עז.", 1 "The abbreviation הריב״א is resolved as the RiVa based on the Or HaChaim's own parenthetical citation of Tosafot Pesachim 96b.", 2 "The word אליה refers to the fatty tail of sheep; the Or HaChaim's argument concerns why the goat's section omits it."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:52:58.343726316Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}